# The national unit for japan. Items take n=2; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g5-japan-our-country-in-its-time v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=9 subject=history nation=japan levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Japan's land and how people live from it"
  goal "Put changes in Japan's life after 1950 in order, and say how fast railways and new machines changed how people work and travel."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=1950 to=1970 step=5 years=[1953, 1955, 1964] labels=["Television broadcasts in Japan", "Electric rice cooker", "Shinkansen opens"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      Japan is a long chain of islands, mostly mountains and forests, with farms, towns and factories crowded on the plains by the sea. In the 1950s new machines came into homes: television broadcasts began in 1953, and the electric rice cooker went on sale in 1955. In 1964 the Shinkansen, the world's first high-speed railway, joined Tokyo and Osaka.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n5-what n=2
    }
    show history.n5-order n=2 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n5-between n=2 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n5-century n=2 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.voyage-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.continent-letter k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.print-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n5-order n=2 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n5-what n=2
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n5-century n=2 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.voyage-order k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n5-between n=2 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n5-why n=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n5-find-out n=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Every date in our country's story can be put on the line and counted from, and every one came from a source."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is to use the grade's methods (timelines across year one, centuries, sources) on a
    chapter of the family's own country. The way in names what a thing was; the core orders three
    events, counts the years between two, names a century, and reviews voyages, continents and
    printing; the two-star question asks why something was done. If the child is stuck on
    counting, count to the next hundred first. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you. Japan's 社会 for grade five aims at 「我が国の国土の様子，国土の環境と国民生活との関連について理解」, the land of Japan and how people live from it (the course of study as read in its 2008 edition).
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the thing was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions are the plainest."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
